The extraction works well, tehre are some issue with the extracting functions that I need to solve.

Most likely causes:
- newer inline XBRL / HTML structure
- headings no longer appearing as clean line starts like ^Item 7
- item headers embedded inside long HTML text blocks
- your regex being too dependent on line-based structure after HTML parsing
So the pipeline is stable now, but extraction quality is still questionable for modern filings.

The biggest issue is that there is a clear year pattern:
- 2010 to around 2019 mostly look good
- 2020 onward, especially 2021 to 2026, many firms flip to:
    bus = no_section_found
    mgmt = no_section_found
That is not random noise. It points to a systematic extraction issue with newer filing formatting.

Most likely causes:
- newer inline XBRL / HTML structure
- headings no longer appearing as clean line starts like ^Item 7
- item headers embedded inside long HTML text blocks
- your regex being too dependent on line-based structure after HTML parsing
So the pipeline is stable now, but extraction quality is still questionable for modern filings.

This is not a computational problem anymore but a text-recognition/ regex pattern extraction problem.
The extraction looks at:
- ^Item\\s{0,}7
- ^Item\\s{0,}8
- ^Item\\s{0,}1
This essentially works best when the parsed text preserves its structure across filing line by line. But
in many recent HTML filing, after parsing, the text may look more like:
- Long flattened text blocks
- Headers not at line start
- ITEM 7. or ITEM 1. merged with surrounding content
- Weird spacing or Unicode characters
So the extraction says no_section_found even though the seciton is there

The main problems are:
ITEM&nbsp;1. / ITEM 1. / Item 1. Business are not always normalized into the same form.
Some filings use title-only headings like OUR BUSINESS with an anchor ITEM_1_OUR_BUSINESS, instead of literal Item 1. Business.
Some lines contain TOC references like See “Item 7...” or link-table rows, which can be mistaken for the real heading.
Old encodings like &#146;, &#147;, &#149; are not being normalized well enough.
Your current regexes are too dependent on exact line starts and exact wording.
What this means for the extractor design
You should keep the Edgar logic, but improve it in three places:
1. Stronger normalization before matching
You need to normalize HTML entities and spacing much more aggressively before section detection.

